  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Daily Tabletop",
    "overview": "Calendar Puzzle is a real puzzle on a quiet table, played once a day. The board and the eight pieces are the objects in the room, and everything else steps back so they can be handled. The mood is calm, tactile, and satisfying: an unhurried ritual where the reward is the feel of a piece landing and the board filling up.\n\nThe system is built from two layers. The object layer holds the board and pieces, which have weight, bevels, shadows, and most of the colour. The interface layer holds toolbars, trays, and dialogs, which stay flat and precise. Trays and dialogs stay neutral, while toolbar buttons use colour to show their job. Density stays moderate, because the puzzle should fill the view.\n\nThe system rejects the generic admin dashboard: a default component-library look of grey toolbars and bordered cards, with no sense of a game.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "The pieces carry most of the colour, on a neutral table.",
      "Toolbar buttons use colour only to show what each button does.",
      "Today's date is the one loud mark on the board.",
      "Physical objects have depth, and controls do not.",
      "Light and dark themes use the same structure and the same piece colours."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Pieces Own the Colour Rule",
        "body": "Saturated colour belongs to the pieces and the date marker. The one exception is toolbar buttons, which use colour to show their job. Every other interface surface stays neutral.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Colour Means a Job Rule",
        "body": "On the toolbar, slate means information about the game, blue means an action on the puzzle, and orange means Reset. A button with no fill is disabled. The date button is the one exception, because its colour shows the date's status: blue when not started, violet when started, green when solved. The admin-only Solution button stays green.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Marker Rule",
        "body": "Date Marker Yellow always means today, and nothing else uses it.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hold It Rule",
        "body": "Only an object you could hold gets a shadow or bevel. Contained buttons have elevation disabled, and shadows on controls are limited to focus rings.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do give saturated colour to the pieces and the date marker, and keep trays and dialogs neutral.",
      "Do use toolbar colour only to show a button's job: slate for information, blue for actions, orange for Reset.",
      "Do keep shadows and bevels on the board and pieces only.",
      "Do make placing a piece feel physical, through weight and contact rather than rewards.",
      "Do use theme tokens (`theme.game.radius.*`, `theme.spacing()`, `theme.palette.*`) rather than raw values, in sibling `.styled.ts` files.",
      "Do keep each piece's colour identical in both themes."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't let the game look like a generic admin dashboard, with grey toolbars and bordered cards and no sense of play.",
      "Don't use Date Marker Yellow for anything except today's cells.",
      "Don't add elevation to buttons, toolbars, or trays.",
      "Don't set button labels in uppercase."
    ]
  }
}
